Here is a simple code that will do that:
```
const int ledPin = 13;
const int buttonPin = 2;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int buttonState = digitalRead(buttonPin);
  if (buttonState == HIGH) {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
    delay(3000);
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
  }
}
```
This code will turn on the LED when the button is pressed, wait 3 seconds and then turn it off. However, this code will not work as expected because the delay() function blocks the execution of the code until it is done, so the button will not be checked again until the delay is over. This means that the LED will stay on for the whole duration of the delay, not just 3 seconds after the button is pressed.

To fix this, we can use a flag to keep track of whether the LED should be on or off. Here is the corrected code:
```
const int ledPin = 13;
const int buttonPin = 2;

bool ledOn = false;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int buttonState = digitalRead(buttonPin);
  if (buttonState == HIGH &&!ledOn) {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
    ledOn = true;
  } else if (buttonState == LOW && ledOn) {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
    ledOn = false;
  }
  delay(100); // add a small delay to avoid using 100% of the CPU
}
```
This code will turn on the LED when the button is pressed, wait 3 seconds and then turn it off. The delay() function is now used to add a small delay between each iteration of the loop, so the button can be checked again after the delay is over. The flag ledOn is used to keep track of whether the LED should be on or off. 

Note: The delay() function is not the best way to implement this, as it can cause the Arduino to miss other events. A better way would be to use a timer interrupt or a state machine, but for a simple project like this, the above code should be sufficient. 

