int main() {
    std::vector<int> items = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    std::srand(std::time(0)); // seed the random number generator

    // iterate through each item in the array in a random order
    for (int i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) {
        int random_index = std::rand() % items.size();
        std::cout << items[random_index] << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code will print the items in the array in a random order, but it's not very efficient because it uses `std::rand()` to generate a random index for each item, which can lead to duplicate indices and inefficient iteration.

A more efficient approach would be to use the Fisher-Yates shuffle algorithm, which is a widely used algorithm for shuffling arrays in a random order. Here's how you can implement it in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <ctime>
#include <cstdlib>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> items = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    std::srand(std::time(0)); // seed the random number generator

    // implement the Fisher-Yates shuffle algorithm
    for (int i = items.size() - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        int j = std::rand() % (i + 1);
        std::swap(items[i], items[j]);
    }

    // iterate through each item in the array in a random order
    for (int i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) {
        std::cout << items[i] << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses the Fisher-Yates shuffle algorithm to shuffle the array in a random order, and then iterates through each item in the array in the shuffled order. This approach is more efficient than the previous one because it only uses `std::rand()` once for each item, rather than once for each iteration. 

